[[!meta title="GDAL/OGR: vector operations"]]
[[!tag gis gdal]]

Part of the [[GDAL/OGR cheat sheet|gdal]].

[[!toc levels=2]]

## Get vector information

```bash
ogrinfo -so input.shp layer-name
```

Or, for all layers:

```bash
ogrinfo -al -so input.shp
```

## Print vector extent

```bash
ogrinfo input.shp layer-name | grep Extent
```

## List vector drivers

```bash
ogr2ogr --formats
```

## Convert between vector formats

```bash
ogr2ogr -f "GeoJSON" output.json input.shp
```

## Print count of features with attributes matching a given pattern

```bash
ogrinfo input.shp layer-name | grep "Search Pattern" | sort | uniq -c
```

## Read from a zip file

This assumes that `archive.zip` is in the current directory.  This example
just extracts the file, but any `ogr2ogr` operation should work.  It's also
possible to write to existing zip files.

```bash
ogr2ogr -f 'GeoJSON' dest.geojson /vsizip/archive.zip/zipped_dir/in.geojson
```

## Clip vectors by bounding box

```bash
ogr2ogr -f "ESRI Shapefile" output.shp input.shp -clipsrc <x_min> <y_min> <x_max> <y_max>
```

## Clip one vector by another

```bash
ogr2ogr -clipsrc clipping_polygon.shp output.shp input.shp
```

## Reproject vector

```bash
ogr2ogr output.shp -t_srs "EPSG:4326" input.shp
```

## Convert MULTIPOLYGON to POLYGON

This assumes that `input.shp` has the largest part as the first geometry for
every multipolygon.  If not, parts need to be removed.

```bash
ogrinfo -dialect SQLite -sql "UPDATE input SET geometry = ST_GeometryN(geometry,1)" input.shp
```

## Convert 3D POLYGONS to 2D POLYGONS

```bash
ogr2ogr -lco "SHPT=POLYGON"  dst-2d.shp  src-3d.shp
```

## Convert an XLSX to a Shapefile

```bash
ogr2ogr -f CSV file.csv file.xlsx
ogr2ogr -a_srs EPSG:4326 -oo X_POSSIBLE_NAMES=Longitude -oo Y_POSSIBLE_NAMES=Latitude  file.shp file.csv

```

## Add an index to a shapefile

Add an index on an attribute:

```bash
ogrinfo example.shp -sql "CREATE INDEX ON example USING fieldname"
```

Add a spatial index:

```bash
ogrinfo example.shp -sql "CREATE SPATIAL INDEX ON example"
```

## Merge features in a vector file by attribute ("dissolve")

```bash
ogr2ogr -f "ESRI Shapefile" dissolved.shp input.shp -dialect sqlite -sql "select ST_union(Geometry),common_attribute from input GROUP BY common_attribute"
```

## Merge features ("dissolve") using a buffer to avoid slivers

```bash
ogr2ogr -f "ESRI Shapefile" dissolved.shp input.shp -dialect sqlite \
-sql "select ST_union(ST_buffer(Geometry,0.001)),common_attribute from input GROUP BY common_attribute"
```

## Merge vector files

```bash
ogr2ogr merged.shp input1.shp
ogr2ogr -update -append merged.shp input2.shp -nln merged
```

## Extract from a vector file based on query

To extract features with `STATENAME` 'New York', 'New Hampshire', etc. from
`states.shp`:

```bash
ogr2ogr -where 'STATENAME like "New%"' states_subset.shp states.shp
```

To extract type 'pond' from `water.shp`:

```bash
ogr2ogr -where "type = pond" ponds.shp water.shp
```

## Subset and filter all shapefiles in a directory

Assumes that the filename and the name of the layer of interest are the
same:

```bash
basename -s.shp *.shp | xargs -n1 -I % ogr2ogr %-subset.shp %.shp -sql "SELECT field-one, field-two FROM '%' WHERE field-one='value-of-interest'"
```

## Extract data from a PostGIS database to a GeoJSON file

```bash
ogr2ogr -f "GeoJSON" file.geojson PG:"host=localhost dbname=database user=user password=password" \
-sql "SELECT * from table_name"
```

## Extract data from an ESRI REST API

Services that use ESRI maps are sometimes powered by a REST server that can
provide data in a form OGR can consume.  Finding the correct end point can
be tricky and may take some false starts.

```bash
ogr2ogr -f GeoJSON output.geojson "http://example.com/arcgis/rest/services/SERVICE/LAYER/MapServer/0/query?f=json&returnGeometry=true&etc=..." OGRGeoJSON
```

## Get the difference between two vector files

Given two files that both have an `id` field, this will produce a vector
file with the part of `file1.shp` that doesn't intersect with `file2.shp`:

```bash
ogr2ogr diff.shp file1.shp -dialect sqlite \
-sql "SELECT ST_Difference(a.Geometry, b.Geometry) AS Geometry, a.id \
FROM file1 a LEFT JOIN 'file2.shp'.file2 b USING (id) WHERE a.Geometry != b.Geometry"
```

This assumes that `file1.shp` and `file2.shp` are both in the current
directory.

## Spatial join

A spatial join transfers properties from one vector layer to another based
on a [spatial
relationship](http://postgis.net/docs/manual-2.0/reference.html#Spatial_Relationships_Measurements)
between the features.  Fields from the join layer can be
[aggregated](https://www.sqlite.org/lang_aggfunc.html) in the output.

Given a set of points (`trees.shp`) and a set of polygons (`parks.shp`) in
the same directory, create a polygon layer with the geometries from
`parks.shp` and summaries of some columns in `trees.shp`:

```bash
ogr2ogr -f 'ESRI Shapefile' output.shp parks.shp -dialect sqlite \
-sql "SELECT p.Geometry, p.id id, SUM(t.field1) field1_sum, AVG(t.field2) field2_avg
FROM parks p, 'trees.shp'.trees t WHERE ST_Contains(p.Geometry, t.Geometry) GROUP BY p.id"
```

Note that features from `parks.shp` that don't overlap with `trees.shp`
won't be in the new file.

## Upload a GeoPackage table to a PostGIS database

```bash
ogr2ogr -progress -f Postgresql pg:"dbname=database user=user password=password port=port" \
-nln new_table_name  geopackage.gpkg -sql "select * from table_in_geopackage"
```
